## 2. Technical Standards & Mathematics

### A. Reoriented Normal Mapping (RNM)
Standard linear addition ($\vec{N}_1 + \vec{N}_2$) flattens surface vectors and creates unnatural shading artifacts. RNM treats the base normal as a rotated reference frame:
$$\vec{N}_1^* = (x_1, y_1, z_1 + 1), \quad \vec{N}_2^* = (-x_2, -y_2, z_2)$$
$$\vec{R} = \vec{N}_1^* \cdot \frac{\vec{N}_1^* \cdot \vec{N}_2^*}{z_1 + 1} - \vec{N}_2^*, \quad \vec{N}_{\text{final}} = \frac{\vec{R}}{\|\vec{R}\|}$$
*Use `game-texture-editor normal-blend` to preserve detail vectors without flattening slopes.*

### B. Alpha Edge Dilation (Texture Bleed)
Texture filtering (bilinear/trilinear interpolation) and mipmap downsampling pull color from adjacent transparent pixels. If transparent pixels are black (`#00000000`), dark halos appear along cutout edges in game engines.
* `game-texture-editor alpha-bleed` dilates border RGB colors outward into empty space while preserving the exact 1-bit or smooth alpha channel.

### C. Albedo De-lighting
Photographed textures contain baked directional lighting and shadow gradients that clash with real-time game lighting engines.
* The frequency separation de-lighter isolates low-frequency illumination ($L_{\text{shading}}$) and divides it out from raw color ($C_{\text{raw}}$), normalizing luminance while preserving high-frequency surface detail:
$$A_{\text{delighted}} = \text{clip}\left(\frac{C_{\text{raw}}}{\max(L_{\text{shading}}, 0.05)} \times 0.5 + 0.25, 0.0, 1.0\right)$$

